  "body": "### What happens\n\nWhen a static subtree is turned into a `createStaticVNode` string, a constant `value` on `<textarea>` or `<select>` is printed as a `value=\"...\"` attribute. Neither element has a `value` content attribute, so after the string is inserted via `innerHTML` the textarea is empty and the select keeps its first option.\n\nWithout stringification the runtime sets `value` as a DOM property (children are mounted first for `<select value>`), so the same template renders correctly. SSR also renders textarea `value` as content. The result therefore depends on whether the surrounding markup reaches the stringify thresholds.\n\n### What should happen\n\n`<textarea :value=\"'hi'\">` shows `hi` and `<select :value=\"'b'\">` selects `b`, whether or not the tree is stringified.\n\n### Reproduction\n\nCompile with `hoistStatic: true` and `prefixIdentifiers: true` (the SFC defaults):\n\n```vue\n<template>\n  <div>\n    <div>\n      <textarea :value=\"'hi'\"></textarea>\n      <select :value=\"'b'\">\n        <option value=\"a\">a</option>\n        <option value=\"b\">b</option>\n      </select>\n      <span class=\"x\"></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</template>\n```\n\nThe inner `<div>` compiles to:\n\n```js\n_createStaticVNode(\"<div><textarea value=\\\"hi\\\"></textarea><select value=\\\"b\\\"><option value=\\\"a\\\">a</option><option value=\\\"b\\\">b</option></select><span class=\\\"x\\\"></span></div>\", 1)\n```\n\nMounted in the browser (or jsdom), `textarea.value` is `\"\"` and `select.value` is `\"a\"`. Removing the `class` from the span (so the tree no longer reaches the threshold) gives `\"hi\"` and `\"b\"`. The plain attribute form `<textarea value=\"hi\">` behaves the same way.\n\n### Root cause\n\n`analyzeNode` in `packages/compiler-dom/src/transforms/stringifyStatic.ts` only checks that an attribute name is a known HTML attribute (the fix for #1128). `value` is a known attribute because of `<input>`, `<option>`, `<button>` and others, so it passes for `<textarea>` and `<select>` too, even though on those elements it only exists as a DOM property. `<option :value>` already has a dedicated bail in the same function.",
